module testapi/scalar;

import testapi(Bool, F64, I32, Scalar, String);

// `testapi/scalar.kio` — the host's **opaque scalar** capability.
//
// `dyn_load_prime`'s value model carries a guest's host scalars opaquely
// (`value.kio` § `V_host`): the interpreter never learns whether a
// `V_host` is an i32, a string, a bool, or a float — it only threads the
// opaque `Scalar` the host owns. The host supplies the whole scalar
// vocabulary here: build one from a literal's text, lift a typed host
// value into the box, project a typed host value back out, and test an
// opaque bool. Adding a new scalar shape (a wider numeric, a host
// function value) is a new lift / project pair here plus the host backing
// it — no interpreter change, because the interpreter names none of these.
//
// `Scalar` is a `role`-less `host type`: an opaque host-owned box, like a
// `Token` or `Box` capability, not a role-bearing primitive. The host
// decides its representation (a tagged union of the scalar shapes); Kio
// never introspects it.

// =====================================================================
// Literal construction — a literal's text + a host representation key → a Scalar
// =====================================================================
//
// A Kio' literal pins a host-typed value with its `(Type)` annotation —
// `42(I32)`, `"hi"(String)`, `.t(Bool)`, `562.406(F64)`. The interpreter
// parses neither the value nor the type: it carries the literal's **raw
// text** and exact resolved host-type descriptor. The fixture's literal
// capability maps that descriptor to a representation key and constructs the
// scalar. So the interpreter knows no scalar representation; the host owns the
// mapping and the text-to-value parse.
//
// The representation key is private to this fixture host. It is selected by
// exact host-type identity, not inferred from the type's role or spelling.
host fn make_scalar(p0: String, p1: String) -> Scalar;

// =====================================================================
// Lifts — a typed host value → the opaque Scalar box
// =====================================================================
//
// A host op's result is a typed host value (`add_i32` yields an `I32`,
// `string_concat` a `String`); lifting it into the opaque box is how the
// host `call` capability returns a scalar to the interpreter without the
// interpreter learning the shape.
host fn scalar_of_i32(p0: I32) -> Scalar;

host fn scalar_of_str(p0: String) -> Scalar;

host fn scalar_of_bool(p0: Bool) -> Scalar;

host fn scalar_of_f64(p0: F64) -> Scalar;

// =====================================================================
// Projections — the opaque Scalar box → a typed host value, or `()`
// =====================================================================
//
// A host op's operand arrives as an opaque `Scalar`; projecting the typed
// host value back out is how the host `call` capability reads it before
// applying the op. The `()` arm covers a scalar of the wrong shape (a
// well-typed guest never triggers it), so a call applier can branch on a
// mismatch rather than fault.
host fn scalar_as_i32(p0: Scalar) -> . | I32;

host fn scalar_as_str(p0: Scalar) -> . | String;

host fn scalar_as_bool(p0: Scalar) -> . | Bool;

host fn scalar_as_f64(p0: Scalar) -> . | F64;

// =====================================================================
// Bool test — the opaque Scalar box → a host bool decision
// =====================================================================
//
// `__if_then_else__` over a guest `Bool` reaches the interpreter as an
// opaque `Scalar`; the interpreter cannot read its truth, so the host
// tests it. The decision (a host `Bool`) enters the interpreter only at
// the `test_bool` capability's conversion site, where it becomes a structural
// choice the interpreter branches on (`hostrec.kio`'s `test_bool_default`).
host fn scalar_is_true(p0: Scalar) -> Bool;
